diff --git a/provisioning/iam/ordinary-principal-boundary.md b/provisioning/iam/ordinary-principal-boundary.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e2e0b95
--- /dev/null
+++ b/provisioning/iam/ordinary-principal-boundary.md
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+This separate successor preserves the sealed v3 producer and its intentional live `global` operation-read check. It adds a genuine persisted ordinary service-account regression, not managed-workload or complete Accounting/Auth/IAM HTTP join acceptance.
+
+Actual Auth `RsaAccessTokenIssuer.IssueService` emits `sub=service:{clientId}`, service kind and permissions, without a GUID `user_id` or NameIdentifier. Pinned Defaults selects `user_id`, then `sub`, then NameIdentifier. Actual IAM `PrincipalService.ResolvePrincipalIdAsync` accepts a GUID or looks up a persisted principal by exact subject email, then `{lowercaseSubject}@serviceaccount.maliev.local`. Ordinary `CreateAsync` creates the real GUID and persists it. The fixture uses that exact supported email enrollment convention for `legacy-auth` and `legacy-quotation`; it does not rewrite a JWT, supply a test-only GUID claim, install a mocked principal resolver or create an alias map.
+
+The owned PostgreSQL fixture applies actual IAM EF migrations. Actual catalogue registration, role registration, ordinary principal creation, global role grant and revocation run through the respective IAM services and PostgreSQL repositories. Twelve source-authored cases use signature-validated tokens from the actual Auth issuer: global access, missing grant, nonmatching scoped grant, revocation, inactive principal and unresolved subject, for each workload. The cache, audit sink and event transport are mocked; principal, permission and binding storage and resolver are real. Every case rolls back its enrollment and bindings. No native execution has occurred.
+
+The current managed workload provisioner uses `{workloadId}@workload.maliev.local` and its default profiles do not include this financial permission. That email is not the existing legacy opaque-subject fallback. The supported deployed enrollment/composition path for these ordinary accounts remains an owner obligation: this fixture establishes source behavior only. It does not prove a managed-workload enrolment, token-exchange profile, genuine live-check HTTP transport, or Accounting financial response. Those are mandatory before full migration acceptance; no live enrollment or binding was applied.
+
+Cleanup supervision first waits for the original outer disposal task to settle. A separately bounded 120-second recovery then waits for the retained original context tasks, requires physical context closure, verifies the exact container ID/image/creation evidence/run labels/disposable mounts/loopback binding and absence of local database clients, and awaits the original container disposal task. It verifies actual Docker 404 absence. Original context and backend disposal calls are not duplicated; a failed recovery may be retried separately after settlement. Even successful physical recovery preserves the first qualification timeout/fault. If context closure or backend absence cannot be verified, qualification remains failed and exact owned container evidence remains available for the enclosing supervisor. Seven controlled source-authored regression cases cover late completion, in-flight timeout, incomplete physical closure, ownership/idle failure, repeated original-one-call cleanup and sticky failure.
+
+Source inputs: Accounting `668f2cb63c64b911db776329b983dd91944b3b8c`; pinned Defaults `7edcd961024868513fd5f373cab3dcb261197f77`; actual IAM `4fe6642e5674013de9a3672505aec898fdcae0ed`; actual Auth `88e430946465a1df6238e10b815d495d43453411`. The standalone project requires explicit reviewed IAM and Auth source roots and their dependency graphs. Its forecast is 23 cases (four catalogue, twelve persisted ordinary-principal and seven cleanup controls); the Accounting suite remains 1653. All builds, tests, raw evidence, static gates and container cleanup verification remain mandatory under the existing memory and sole-phase admission guard.
